T-1000-Detail [f37c735bb449] <<LAND225>> The ticket's fix shape (a length bound on the chat message and a body limit for that route, refused with an existing answer) and its done-when, read against the independent review and the builder's record. (1) The change is 32 added lines in backend/server.js. CHAT_MESSAGE_MAX_CHARS is 100000 (line 1424) and the check refuses a string longer than that, counting its length in UTF-16 code units (line 1428); the refusal sits directly after each route's own missing-message check, at lines 7086, 7575 and 7774, and on the two stream routes it comes before the stream is opened. The comparison is greater-than, so exactly 100000 is answered: the review measured 200 at 100000 and 400 at 100001, and 200 for 50000 astral characters (100000 units) and 400 for 50001. (2) The 2 MB cap is a pair of JSON and form parsers mounted on the /api/chat prefix (line 1430) after the rate limiter (line 1388) and above the global 50 MB parsers (lines 1446 and 1449); body-parser skips a body that is already read, so a chat body over 2 MB answers 413 before any chat route runs and every other route keeps 50 MB. The review's rows for this (a JSON body, a form body, a charset form and a mixed-case form of the content type, three compressions that inflate past 2 MB, a chunked body, five path variants) read 413 for a big body and 400 for a long message; four other content types (a vendor JSON type, plain text, a byte stream and none) read 500 with no model call, because neither parser reads such a body; every row made 0 model calls, and no request reached a chat handler with a body over 2 MB. (3) The done-when, measured: with a 40 MB message the server's health answer took 4741 ms and 4927 ms at the base and 11 ms and 10 ms at the head in the builder's runs (the review read 4770 ms at the base and 12 ms and 11 ms at the head); an ordinary message answers 200 as before (cell N1); the new test file holds eleven cells, R1 to R7 red at the base and all eleven green at the head (twice in the review); the full verify at the head read 5898 of 5898 tests across 379 suites. (4) Plants: the review ran eight of the builder's plants (P1 to P5, P7, P8 and P11; it did not run P6, P9 and P10) and each turned red the cells the builder's matrix lists; cell R6 turns red on time only under the plant that removes both the cap and the message check (4824 ms in the review), and under the two plants that send the big request to a 400 it reads red on its outcome check and not on time. Not plant-proved, by the builder's own account: the 2 MB number, the 2000 ms bound of R6 and cell N4. Findings of the review, none blocking: a sentence in one commit message says an oversized body is never read in full, which is not exact (the parser reads off and discards the rest of an over-cap body before answering 413, and never buffers or parses it); the 413 answer is generic (an error text of An error occurred, because the error handler's table of safe messages has no entry for 413); cell N3 asserts only that the answer is not 413 and not null (a plant still turns it red); a message that is not a string is not refused by the new check, as designed (no model call for any such shape in the review's probes, and the size is bounded by the 2 MB cap), and the review recommends a follow-up ticket for it; and one process note of the reviewer's own (two lock acquisitions). One observation outside this change: in the review's harness (development setting, open mode) POST /api/chat/clear-cache answered 200 to a request with no sign-in, at the base and at the head. RD-964 owns the rule for that route, so a separate note on RD-964 passes the review's row on and asks its owner to verify on main; this note does not decide whether it is a regression. Not measured: any hosted instance, Node 24 and Linux beyond the hosted Build, the browser rendering of anything, the 2 MB number against real chat use, and the demo (the Deploy demo run read skipped on this merge). [LAND-225-1